y the previous player** (BuGang indicates an added tile to form a kong), so no more F4 available.\\n\\nCrucially, **you cannot win with a pure lower-tile hand if you keep both terminals (T1 and T9)** unless they form part of valid chows. But:\\n- T1 can only form a chow with T2-T3 (but T3 is exhausted).\\n- T9 would need T7-T8 for a chow, but T7 is gone, T8 is down to 1 (and next player may be waiting on it).\\n\\nMoreover, you already have a **pung of F1 (East Wind)**, which gives you **Dragon Pung? No \\u2014 F1 is wind, not dragon**. But you do get **Prevalent Wind (South = F2)** and **Seat Wind (West = F3)** as scoring patterns if you have those pungs. However, you don\\u2019t have F2 or F3.\\n\\nBut note: you **Peng\\u2019d F1**, so that\\u2019s an exposed pung of East Wind. That doesn't help Seat or Prevalent Wind scoring.\\n\\nLet\\u2019s assess potential patterns:\\n\\n- **All Pungs (6 points)**: You have 2 pungs (F1, B1), 2 chows \\u2192 not applicable.\\n- **Outside Hand (4 points)**: Requires every meld to contain terminal or honor. Your chows [T2,T3,T4] and [B2,B3,B4] are internal numbers \\u2192 not outside.\\n- **Half Flush (6 points)**: Could aim for all-character or all-dot flush? No \\u2014 you have mixed suits and honors.\\n- **Lower Tiles (24 points)**: All tiles 1\\u20133. You have strong components: [T2,T3,T4] (almost), [B2,B3,B4], [B1,B1,B1], [F1,F1,F1]. But T4 and B4 are number 4 \\u2014 too high! So this breaks Lower Tiles.\\n- **Lower Four (12 points)**: Allows 1\\u20134. Yes! This pattern qualifies: all suited tiles are 1\\u20134. You have:\\n  - Melds: [T2,T3,T4], [B2,B3,B4], [B1,B1,B1]\\n  - Remaining: T1 and T9\\n  - If you discard T9, and draw T1 or another low tile to make a pair (e.g., T1 pair?), but you only have one T1 now.\\n\\nWait: after discarding T9, your hand becomes: T1 + unknown drawn tile.\\n\\nBut you need a pair.\\n\\nPossible pairs?\\n- Already used: F1 (pung), B1 (pung), T2/T3/T4/B2/B3/B4 \\u2014 all in chows/pungs.\\n- Available singles: T1, T9.\\n\\nSo only way to get a pair is to draw a duplicate of a loose tile.\\n\\nBut **T1 still has 1 unshown** (since total 4 copies: who has them?) \\u2014 let's estimate:\\n- Total T1: 4 copies.\\n- Unshown: 1 \\u2192 so 3 already visible/discharged.\\n- You have 1 \\u2192 so 3 others played or melded.\\n- Next player discarded T1 once, across discarded T1 once, previous didn\\u2019t show T1 \\u2192 possibly only two seen \\u2192 maybe 1 left?\\n\\nStill, slim chance.\\n\\nAlternatively, could you go for **All Types (6 points)**? Need all three suits, winds, dragons.\\n\\nYou have:\\n- Characters? None in hand or melds.\\n- You\\u2019ve discarded W1, W4, W6, W8, W9 \\u2014 so you had some, but no meld in characters.\\n- Do you have any character tile now? No.\\n\\nSo missing a full suit.\\n\\nThus, **no Full Flush**, **no Pure Straight**, etc.\\n\\nBack to **discarding decision**:\\n\\nOption 1: **Play T9**\\n- Keeps T1, which fits better with current structure: you have [T2,T3,T4], so T1-T2-T3 would overlap, but T3 is exhausted (0 left). Impossible to complete [T1,T2,T3].\\n- But T1 is still part of the \\\"low number\\\" theme and less disruptive to future pairing? Not really.\\n\\nOption 2: **Play T1**\\n- Leaves T9 \\u2014 but T9 is isolated. Needs T7-T8 for chow \\u2014 T7 is 0 left, so impossible.\\n- T9 cannot form any legal chow now.\\n\\nHence, **T9 is useless**. It cannot complete any chow due to missing T7/T8.\\n\\nWhereas T1, although T3 is gone, **could theoretically pair with itself** if you draw another T1 (still 1 unshown), or be part of a hypothetical [T1,T2,T3] if someone had discarded earlier \\u2014 but no.\\n\\nBut wait: **you already have T2,T3,T4** \\u2014 that\\u2019s fixed. So T1 is just a singleton.\\n\\nSame with T9.\\n\\nSo both are dead ends.\\n\\nBut here\\u2019s a key point: **you already have two chows in the 2-3-4 range** ([T2,T3,T4], [B2,B3,B4]) and a pung of B1 \\u2014 very close to a **Mixed Shifted Chows** pattern? No, that requires three shifted chows across suits.\\n\\nBut you also have **F1 pung** \\u2014 honor.\\n\\nBest bet: **minimize disruption**.\\n\\nBut consider safety.\\n\\nWho might be waiting?\\n\\nLook at what others have discarded.\\n\\nThe **previous player** has:\\n- Melds: [W9,W9,W9], [F4,F4,F4,F4], [B1,B2,B3]\\n- They completed a kong on F4 \\u2014 dangerous tile.\\n- Also, they Chi\\u2019d B2 \\u2014 showing interest in bamboo.\\n\\nThey may be going for **Half Flush** in Dots or something else.\\n\\nNext player has:\\n- [W3,W4,W5], [T9,T9,T9], [W6,W7,W8] \\u2192 clearly going for middle/upper characters, possibly **Full Flush** in Characters.\\n\\nThey just Chi\\u2019d W7 \\u2014 so completing upper end.\\n\\nThey discarded T8 twice \\u2014 possibly safe.\\n\\nBut you don\\u2019t have T8.\\n\\nAcross player has:\\n- [B6,B7,B8], [W4,W5,W6] \\u2192 mid-high dots and middle characters.\\n\\nNow, **T1 and T9** \\u2014 both are terminals.\\n\\nBut **T9**: next player has a **pung of T9** already (Peng T9)! So they have three T9s. Only one left unshown (T9:1). So if you discard T9, it will complete their **kong**? Possibly.\\n\\nBut more importantly \\u2014 **could they be waiting to win on T9**?\\n\\nUnlikely \\u2014 they already have three T9s. To win on T9, they\\u2019d need a pair and then claim T9 as the fourth \\u2014 but that would be for a kong, not a win unless it completes a pair-based wait.\\n\\nBut if they have a hand like:\\n- [T9,T9,T9], [W3,W4,W5], [W6,W7,W8], [W3,W4,W5] \\u2192 no, not possible.\\n\\nMore likely, they are going for **Upper Tiles** or **Middle Tiles** in Characters.\\n\\nBut they have no other Bamboos.\\n\\nSo probably **not waiting on T9**.\\n\\nHowever, **you have a pung of B1**, and **previous player has a chow [B1,B2,B3]** \\u2014 so they used B1.\\n\\nAlso, **across player discarded B9, B8, B7, B6, B2** \\u2014 so not holding low bamboos.\\n\\nNo sign of anyone collecting T1.\\n\\nBut here's a critical insight:\\n\\nYou have **already discarded T7 twice**, and others have too \\u2014 T7 is 0 left \\u2192 exhausted.\\n\\nSimilarly, **W5 is 0**, **B1 is 0** \\u2014 all gone.\\n\\nNow, back to **your own winning chances**.\\n\\nYou have four melds? No \\u2014 you have three melds and two single tiles \\u2192 total 14 tiles? Wait.\\n\\nWait: standard hand is **4 melds + 1 pair = 14 tiles**.\\n\\nEach meld is 3 tiles, pair is 2 \\u2192 4\\u00d73 + 2 = 14.\\n\\nCurrently:\\n- Melds: 3 melds \\u00d7 3 = 9 tiles\\n- Hand: 2 tiles (T1, T9)\\n\\u2192 Total 11 tiles? That can\\u2019t be.\\n\\nWait \\u2014 **error in state**?\\n\\nNo: in Mahjong, when you form a meld, it is exposed, and your hand reduces by 2 tiles (for Peng/Chi) or 3 (for concealed kong).\\n\\nStart with 13 tiles.\\n\\nYou perform 3 melds:\\n- Peng F1: used 2 F1 from hand + 1 claimed \\u2192 cost 2 tiles\\n- Chi T3: used 2 tiles from hand + 1 claimed \\u2192 cost 2 tiles\\n- Peng B1: used 2 B1 from hand + 1 claimed \\u2192 cost 2 tiles\\n\\nSo total tiles removed from hand via melding: 6 tiles.\\n\\nInitial: 13\\nAfter 3 melds: 13 - 6 = 7 tiles in hand\\nThen you drew/discarded many times.\\n\\nBut according to history: you played many tiles \\u2014 Play W5, J1, B2, J2, W9, B6, T9, W6, F2, F4, W1, T7, T7, F3, T6, T4, W8, W1, T1, etc.\\n\\nBut finally, you have **only two tiles left**: T1 and T9.\\n\\nAnd **three melds**.\\n\\nSo total: 3\\u00d73 + 2 = 11 tiles \\u2192 missing 3 tiles.\\n\\nAh! Unless one of the melds is a **kong**?\\n\\nCheck: did you Gang?\\n\\nIn history: you have **Peng B1**, but no **Gang** action.\\n\\nOthers: previous player did **BuGang F4** (added to a pong to make kong).\\n\\nBut you did not.\\n\\nSo you have exactly **three melds (all triplets)** and **two single tiles** \\u2192 only 11 tiles.\\n\\nImpossible.\\n\\nUnless... **one of your melds is listed incorrectly**?\\n\\nYour melds: [[F1, F1, F1],[T2, T3, T4],[B2, B3, B4],[B1, B1, B1]]\\n\\nThat\\u2019s **four melds**!\\n\\nAh! Yes \\u2014