ely, **no one is waiting for T9 to win**.\\n\\nBut if you discard T9, **next player can add it to their kong**? But they haven't declared a concealed kong \\u2014 only Peng.\\n\\nThey can **BuGang** later, but that risks **Robbing the Kong**.\\n\\nBut still, giving them a kong tile helps them score.\\n\\nSimilarly, **T1** \\u2014 who wants it?\\n\\nYou have one.\\n\\nNext player discarded one.\\n\\nAcross discarded one.\\n\\nOne left.\\n\\nNo one has a pung of T1.\\n\\nSo no one can kong it.\\n\\nTherefore, **both discards are relatively safe**.\\n\\nBut now, strategic value:\\n\\nYou want to **maximize your chance to win**.\\n\\nYou will discard one, keep one, then draw.\\n\\nIf you draw the same as the kept tile, you can win (by discarding the other? No \\u2014 you draw, then can win immediately if it makes a pair).\\n\\nWait: current hand after discard: 1 tile in hand, 12 in melds \\u2192 13 tiles.\\n\\nThen you draw \\u2192 14th tile.\\n\\nIf the drawn tile matches the single tile in hand, you now have a pair \\u2192 winning hand.\\n\\nSo yes.\\n\\nSo probability is equal for both T1 and T9 (1 unshown each).\\n\\nBut consider **scoring potential**.\\n\\nIf you win with **T1 pair**, what patterns apply?\\n\\n- You have:\\n  - Two chows in 2-3-4: [T2,T3,T4], [B2,B3,B4]\\n  - Pung of B1 (terminal)\\n  - Pung of F1 (wind)\\n\\nThis hand is mixed.\\n\\nBut **T1 is a terminal**, so having a **pair of terminals** might help **Outside Hand**? No \\u2014 Outside Hand requires every meld to contain terminal or honor.\\n\\nBut your chows [T2,T3,T4] and [B2,B3,B4] are **not** terminal (T2, B2, etc. are not 1 or 9) \\u2192 so **not Outside Hand**.\\n\\nSimilarly, **Lower Four (12 points)**: allows 1-4 \\u2192 yes! All your suited tiles are 1-4.\\n\\nYou have:\\n- B1 (1), B2-B3-B4 (2-3-4), T2-T3-T4 (2-3-4) \\u2192 all 1-4.\\n\\nAnd honors (F1).\\n\\nSo **Lower Four** (12 points) applies.\\n\\nAdditionally:\\n- Dragon Pung? No dragons.\\n- Prevalent Wind? South = F2 \\u2014 you don\\u2019t have it.\\n- Seat Wind? West = F3 \\u2014 you don\\u2019t have it.\\n- You have F1 (East) \\u2014 not scoring.\\n\\nBut **Pung of Terminals or Honors**: F1 is wind \\u2192 yes (2 points)\\n- And B1 is terminal \\u2192 yes (2 points) \\u2192 can count twice?\\n\\nPattern says: \\\"can be counted more than once if there are multiple melds\\\"\\n\\nSo:\\n- [F1,F1,F1]: pung of honor \\u2192 +2\\n- [B1,B1,B1]: pung of terminal \\u2192 +2\\n\\u2192 Total 4 points from this pattern alone.\\n\\nAlso:\\n- **All Pungs?** No \\u2014 you have two chows.\\n- **All Chows?** No \\u2014 you have two pungs.\\n\\nBut **Melded Hand?** All melds are exposed (Peng, Chi, Peng) \\u2192 yes, all from others \\u2192 6 points.\\n\\nBut you need to win by claiming the last tile.\\n\\nIf you win by self-draw, you get **Self Drawn (1 point)** instead.\\n\\nBut **Melded Hand (6 points)** requires:\\n> Every meld and pair in the hand must be completed by tiles from other players, which means that all melds must be exposed, and one tile of the pair is the 14th winning tile from other players.\\n\\nSo you must win by **claiming a discard** to complete the pair.\\n\\nBut right now, you have no pair \\u2014 you will have a single tile after discard.\\n\\nThen, to win, you can either:\\n- Draw the matching tile \\u2192 Self Drawn (1 point)\\n- Or, if someone discards it, you can claim \\u2192 then Melded Hand (6 points) applies.\\n\\nBut Melded Hand is worth more.\\n\\nBut you cannot control that.\\n\\nBack to choice.\\n\\nEither discard leads to same structure.\\n\\nBut **T9 is higher risk** because next player has a pung of T9 \\u2014 they may BuGang on it, and if you discard it, they can promote and score.\\n\\nAlso, **T9 is less thematic** \\u2014 your whole hand is low numbers (1-4), while T9 is 9 \\u2014 outlier.\\n\\nSo **keeping T1 fits the Lower Four pattern better**.\\n\\nEven though both are terminals, T1 belongs to the 1-4 group.\\n\\nThus, **discard T9**, keep T1.\\n\\nThen hope to draw the last T1.\\n\\nFinal evaluation:\\n\\n- Both discards safe.\\n- T9 discard risks helping opponent complete kong.\\n- T1 keeps consistency with \\\"Lower Four\\\" theme.\\n- Winning with T1 pair allows better narrative for scoring (though actual fans don't change based on which terminal).\\n\\nBut no difference in fan count.\\n\\nOnly difference: **T9 might be safer to discard** because next player may want it for kong, but since they didn\\u2019t declare concealed kong, they may not care.\\n\\nBut still, best to avoid feeding.\\n\\nConclusion: **discard T9**, keep T1.\\n\\nReason: T9 is inconsistent with the low-number (1-4) structure of the hand and poses a slight risk of aiding the next player's kong. Keeping T1 aligns better with the \\\"Lower Four\\\" pattern and avoids feeding opponents.\\n\\nAnswer: Play T9\"}"}
