 one.\nNo indication of multiple.\n\nSo no one has three W4s \u2014 so no kong risk.\n\nSimilarly, T1:\n- Who might want T1?\n- I have [T2,T3,T4] \u2014 one T1.\n- Others:\n  - Next: has [T9,T9,T9], and chow [W3,W4,W5] etc \u2014 no T1.\n  - Across: no T1.\n  - Previous: has [B3,B4,B5], [B1,B2,B3] \u2014 no T1.\n- And I discarded T1 earlier \u2014 no one claimed.\n\nSo T1 is very safe.\n\nW4 is also safe.\n\nBut I should keep the tile that gives me better chance to win.\n\nCurrently, I have no pair.\n\nI need to draw a duplicate of W4 or T1 to win.\n\nEqual probability.\n\nBut T1 is a terminal, so slightly more valuable for patterns like Outside Hand or Terminal waits.\n\nBut I have chows with 2,3,4 \u2014 not terminals.\n\nEdge wait: if I am waiting on T1 to complete 1-2-3, but I have 2-3-4, not 1-2-3.\n\nSo not.\n\nClosed wait: if I had T1 and T3, waiting for T2 \u2014 but I don't.\n\nSo no.\n\nBest to discard the one less likely to be needed.\n\nSince I discarded T1 earlier and no one claimed, and I have a T2-3-4 chow, keeping T1 doesn't help much.\n\nW4 is alone.\n\nBut same.\n\nPerhaps keep W4 because I have no other W tiles, but it doesn't matter.\n\nAnother idea: I have a pung of F1 (East), and prevalent wind is South (F2), seat wind is West (F3), so F1 is not scoring.\n\nNo benefit.\n\nUltimately, both are safe, but T1 is a terminal, and terminals are often kept for endgame.\n\nBut I already have a terminal pung (B1), so adding T1 pair might help for All Terminals and Honors, but I have T2,T3,T4 \u2014 not terminals.\n\nB2,B3,B4 \u2014 not.\n\nSo not.\n\nI think either is fine.\n\nBut since the valid actions are only two, and I must choose, I'll go with discarding W4, as it has been more heavily used by opponents, so even safer.\n\nOr T1.\n\nLet's see the history: I played T1 earlier \u2014 so I've already discarded it, and now I have it again? No.\n\nMy history: \"Play T1\" \u2014 I played it once.\n\nThen later, \"Play T1\" again? Let's see: \"Play W1, Play T7, Play T7, Play F3, Play T6, Play T4, Play W8, Play W1, Chi B3, Play T1, Peng B1, Play T9.\"\n\nSo I played T1 once.\n\nThen after that, I Peng B1, then Play T9.\n\nSo I had T1, then discarded it.\n\nNow, my hand is W4, T1 \u2014 so I must have drawn T1 back? Possible.\n\nSo I discarded it once, but now have it again.\n\nSince I discarded it before and no one claimed, it's very safe.\n\nSimilarly, W4: I never discarded it? History: I played W5, W9, W6, W1, W8, W1 \u2014 no W4.\n\nSo I haven't discarded W4.\n\nBut others have.\n\nSo W4 is also safe.\n\nBut since I have only one opportunity, and both are similar, I'll choose to keep T1 and discard W4, because T1 is a terminal, and might be part of a pattern like Chicken Hand or something.\n\nBut no.\n\nPerhaps for Last Tile Claim, but not.\n\nI think the safest is to discard W4.\n\nFinal decision.\n\nAnswer: Play W4\n\nBut let's box it.\n\nEvaluation: Both W4 and T1 are safe discards given their usage in opponents' melds and prior discards. W4 has been used in chows by the next and across players, and T1 was previously discarded by me without being claimed. Neither tile is likely to complete an opponent's winning hand. Keeping T1 offers a slight advantage as it is a terminal tile, potentially contributing to patterns like Outside Hand or All Terminals and Honors if paired, though current melds limit such prospects. Discarding W4 is marginally safer due to higher exposure.\n\nReason: W4 has been extensively used in opponents' completed chows (next player's [W3,W4,W5] and across player's [W4,W5,W6]), making it very unlikely to be needed. T1, while also safe, is a terminal and slightly more valuable if a pair is formed. However, the immediate safety of W4 justifies its discard.\n\nAnswer: Play W4"}
